00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.300
As you've learned in the course
materials, there may be occasions

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.490
where you need to work with CSV files.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.260
CSVs are simply files that can be
accessed by other software applications.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.620
So for example, you may have information
in a spreadsheet that you need to

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.669
import into a database, and you would
save the Excel spreadsheet as a CSV

00:00:18.669 --> 00:00:23.250
file, which would then enable the
database to be able to access it.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.510
Accountancy software is another
example of software that uses CSV

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.610
and that can be exported and imported
from Excel and to Excel and from

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.739
the software packages that you need.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.660
So just to show an example of
this, I have here a spreadsheet

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.840
which is already formatted, I've
just called that CSV example.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.430
But as you can see, this has
a range of formatting applied.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.000
So it has been formatted
and it looks presentable.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:58.050
If I now go to File and Save As
and then select, rather than an

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:03.120
Excel workbook, if I select a CSV.

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:04.739
So comma delimited.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.380
So that quite simply means that
it is a comma separated value.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:15.285
And if I save that now and I
then close the example, which

00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:18.105
has got the formatting applied.

00:01:18.585 --> 00:01:21.225
As you can see, I have two files
now I have the original example,

00:01:21.225 --> 00:01:22.695
which was an Excel worksheet.

00:01:23.235 --> 00:01:26.985
And this one here, which is the
Excel comma separated value sheet.

00:01:26.995 --> 00:01:31.135
So if I double click on that and
open that, you will see that it

00:01:31.155 --> 00:01:34.645
has saved it but it has primarily
saved it just the information.

00:01:34.645 --> 00:01:38.355
It has took off all of the formatting,
even the columns are now no longer

00:01:38.355 --> 00:01:40.245
wide enough to display the information.

00:01:40.785 --> 00:01:43.665
So that is what saving as a CSV file does.

00:01:43.755 --> 00:01:45.675
It only keeps the information.

00:01:46.185 --> 00:01:50.575
An example of this is if I just
close that and just say Save,

00:01:51.505 --> 00:01:55.995
if I then go to a Word document.

00:01:56.235 --> 00:01:57.405
So if I open Word.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.900
And this will give you a good
example of how it is structured.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:09.210
If I open the file the CSV
example, you can see that that is

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.460
how the document has been saved.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:17.280
One of the things there is if I actually
made a change in Word, so if I changed

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.670
that to 88, and then just saved that.

00:02:26.985 --> 00:02:29.175
So once I've saved it, I can close that.

00:02:29.805 --> 00:02:36.565
If I then go back to CSV and open the
spreadsheet example, you can see that it

00:02:36.585 --> 00:02:38.235
has actually changed the figure in there.

00:02:38.235 --> 00:02:41.915
So I can edit it in other software
packages, so I could edit the CSV

00:02:41.915 --> 00:02:46.215
file in Word, and then that can
be transferred back into Excel.

